.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt BIO_CONNECT 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BIO_socket ,
.Nm BIO_bind ,
.Nm BIO_connect ,
.Nm BIO_listen ,
.Nm BIO_accept_ex ,
.Nm BIO_closesocket
.Nd BIO socket communication setup routines
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/bio.h
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_socket
.Fa "int domain"
.Fa "int socktype"
.Fa "int protocol"
.Fa "int options"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_bind
.Fa "int sock"
.Fa "const BIO_ADDR *addr"
.Fa "int options"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_connect
.Fa "int sock"
.Fa "const BIO_ADDR *addr"
.Fa "int options"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_listen
.Fa "int sock"
.Fa "const BIO_ADDR *addr"
.Fa "int options"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_accept_ex
.Fa "int accept_sock"
.Fa "BIO_ADDR *peer"
.Fa "int options"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_closesocket
.Fa "int sock"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn BIO_socket
creates a socket in the domain
.Fa domain ,
of type
.Fa socktype
and
.Fa protocol .
Socket
.Fa options
are currently unused, but is present for future use.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_bind
binds the source address and service to a socket and may be useful
before calling
.Fn BIO_connect .
The options may include
.Sy BIO_SOCK_REUSEADDR ,
which is described in
.Sx FLAGS
below.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_connect
connects
.Fa sock
to the address and service given by
.Fa addr .
Connection
.Fa options
may be zero or any combination of
.Sy BIO_SOCK_KEEPALIVE ,
.Sy BIO_SOCK_NONBLOCK
and
.Sy BIO_SOCK_NODELAY .
The flags are described in
.Sx FLAGS
below.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_listen
has
.Fa sock
start listening on the address and service given by
.Fa addr .
Connection
.Fa options
may be zero or any combination of
.Sy BIO_SOCK_KEEPALIVE ,
.Sy BIO_SOCK_NONBLOCK ,
.Sy BIO_SOCK_NODELAY ,
.Sy BIO_SOCK_REUSEADDR
and
.Sy BIO_SOCK_V6_ONLY .
The flags are described in
.Sx FLAGS
below.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_accept_ex
waits for an incoming connections on the given socket
.Fa accept_sock .
When it gets a connection, the address and port of the peer gets stored
in
.Fa peer
if that one is non-NULL. Accept
.Fa options
may be zero or
.Sy BIO_SOCK_NONBLOCK ,
and is applied on the accepted socket.
The flags are described in
.Sx FLAGS
below.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_closesocket
closes
.Fa sock .
.Sh FLAGS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It BIO_SOCK_KEEPALIVE
Enables regular sending of keep-alive messages.
.It BIO_SOCK_NONBLOCK
Sets the socket to nonblocking mode.
.It BIO_SOCK_NODELAY
Corresponds to
.Sy TCP_NODELAY ,
and disables the Nagle algorithm.
With this set, any data will be sent as soon as possible instead of
being buffered until there's enough for the socket to send out in one
go.
.It BIO_SOCK_REUSEADDR
Try to reuse the address and port combination for a recently closed
port.
.It BIO_SOCK_V6_ONLY
When creating an IPv6 socket, make it only listen for IPv6 addresses and
not IPv4 addresses mapped to IPv6.
.It BIO_SOCK_TFO
Enables TCP Fast Open on the socket.
Uses appropriate APIs on supported operating systems, including Linux,
macOS and FreeBSD. Can be used with
.Fn BIO_connect ,
.Xr BIO_set_conn_mode 3 ,
.Xr BIO_set_bind_mode 3 ,
and
.Fn BIO_listen .
On Linux kernels before 4.14, use
.Xr BIO_set_conn_address 3
to specify the peer address before starting the TLS handshake.
.El
.Pp
These flags are bit flags, so they are to be combined with the
.Qo Li | Qc
operator, for example:
.Bd -literal
 BIO_connect(sock, addr, BIO_SOCK_KEEPALIVE | BIO_SOCK_NONBLOCK);
.Ed
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn BIO_socket
returns the socket number on success or
.Sy INVALID_SOCKET
(-1) on error.
When an error has occurred, the OpenSSL error stack will hold the error
data and errno has the system error.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_bind ,
.Fn BIO_connect
and
.Fn BIO_listen
return 1 on success or 0 on error.
When an error has occurred, the OpenSSL error stack will hold the error
data and errno has the system error.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_accept_ex
returns the accepted socket on success or
.Sy INVALID_SOCKET
(-1) on error.
When an error has occurred, the OpenSSL error stack will hold the error
data and errno has the system error.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr BIO_ADDR 3
.Sh HISTORY
.Xr BIO_gethostname 3 ,
.Xr BIO_get_port 3 ,
.Xr BIO_get_host_ip 3 ,
.Xr BIO_get_accept_socket 3
and
.Xr BIO_accept 3
were deprecated in OpenSSL 1.1.0. Use the functions described above
instead.
